{
"A fish breathes with its gills.": "39ef1d3e8a4e",
"A little oxygen dissolves in water.": "0b605000d38b",
"Blood carries oxygen around the body.": "61f030882421",
"Fish need oxygen for the same reason that you do. Their cells use it to get energy from food. There is oxygen dissolved in water, but not much. A liter of fresh water holds only about 8 cubic centimeters of oxygen, and a liter of air holds about 210. Water is also much heavier and thicker than air, so it is hard work to move.": "fff03b978a1c",
"Gills are built to make the most of that little bit. Under the gill cover there are rows of soft red threads. Each thread carries many thin plates, and each plate is full of tiny blood vessels. Together they give the water a very large surface to touch, and the wall between the water and the blood is extremely thin.": "002b2a7e2aca",
"Here is the clever part. In a gill, the blood flows in the opposite direction to the water. Fresh water first meets blood that is nearly full, and gives it a little more. As the water moves on, it meets blood that is emptier and emptier. All the way along, the water has slightly more oxygen than the blood beside it, so the oxygen never stops crossing.": "cb94b497e495",
"Only a thin wall separates water and blood.": "f1015934d93b",
"Oxygen always moves from where there is more of it to where there is less. So when water rich in oxygen passes blood that is poor in oxygen, the oxygen crosses the wall into the blood.": "5ed7178bab6a",
"The water flows one way and the blood the other.": "178cf4f81740",
"They move in opposite directions.": "622ebeeb8095",
"This is why a fish dies in air, even though air has much more oxygen. Out of the water, the soft plates of the gills collapse and stick together, and almost no surface is left for the oxygen to cross.": "0b06b52350b9",
"Watch a fish in a tank. Its mouth opens and closes all day long, as if it were drinking. What it is really doing is breathing. Each gulp sends water across its gills and out through a slit behind its head.": "a3370f62583f",
